Two, one. let's start the fun.

113
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:06,360
So this is Bob Mazzucchi,

114
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:07,360
scout executive of the Boy Scouts,

115
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,360
and you're listening to my buddy, Clark Green,

116
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,360
on the scout master podcast

117
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:13,360
and he is doing a fantastic job:

118
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:17,360
Scout master ship in seven minutes or less.

119
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:27,360
Well, you've gone and done it.

120
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,360
Somebody asked you to be a scout master

121
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:30,360
and now you're a scout master.

122
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:31,360
So what do you need?

123
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:33,360
Well, first and foremost, you need to get trained.

124
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,360
We're going to talk about that later on in another podcast.

125
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:38,360
but training very, very important,

126
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,360
And you also need to be able to supplement that training

127
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,360
by your own reading and study.

128
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:46,360
You're going to get just so much out of going to a training event.

129
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:50,360
You're going to need to think and study your way through some things

130
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:52,360
and then you're also, you know,

131
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:57,360
going to benefit greatly from experience as you go. as you go by.

132
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:00,360
Most of the time when scouting goes awry

133
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:04,360
it's because we adults are ignorant of the program

134
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:06,360
or we just misunderstand the way something works.

135
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:09,360
You'll learn how the program works fairly quickly

136
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:12,360
and spend as many years as you like

137
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:15,360
perfecting your skills at presenting it.

138
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:17,360
It's like a game of golf: moments to learn

139
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:19,360
and a lifetime to master.

140
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:24,360
You need to know confidently what is and what is not good. scouting

141
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,360
Troops develop their own way of doing things over time

142
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:30,360
and if you're taking over the scout master position in a troop,

143
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:34,360
you know- I haven't seen a troop yet, including my own-

144
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,360
They couldn't use some improvement and adjustment

145
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:39,360
as far as program delivery is involved.

146
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,360
When I say program delivery, I mean:

147
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:43,360
why are you doing this?

148
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:44,360
What are you doing?

149
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:46,360
Why are you involved in scouting?

150
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:48,360
You know these are all questions

151
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:50,360
that you have to ask over and over again.

152
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,360
You have to make that program prove itself.

153
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:55,360
You have to take everything that you do as a troop

154
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:57,360
and you have to examine it

155
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:59,360
and you have to say, well, does this really work?

156
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:00,360
Is this really working

157
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:03,360
or is this just an old tradition that we have?

158
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:05,360
I encourage you to study and to learn

159
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,360
and to apply your knowledge,

160
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:09,360
And I think you need to be courageous enough

161
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:11,360
to change your mind when you learn something new.

162
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:13,360
I know that's happened to me several times

163
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,360
and I've had to. you know, let things go

164
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,360
that sometimes we're closely held

165
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:22,360
and venerated traditions and ways of thinking,

166
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,360
But when you learn something new,

167
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,360
you know it's time to change

168
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,360
And that can be hard for us guys.

169
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:30,360
It can be hard for us guys

170
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:33,360
especially if you're, you know, past the age of 45 or 50.

171
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:37,360
It becomes even more difficult

172
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:40,360
Now. it's tempting to substitute our own judgment

173
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,360
for 102 years of experience,

174
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,360
and that's what scouting has.

175
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:47,360
You know it takes a bit of an ego to be a scoutmaster

176
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:51,360
And we like the way that we think

177
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:53,360
and we like our own judgment sometimes.

178
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:56,360
But when we come up against something in the program

179
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:59,360
that is counter to our own judgment

180
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:02,360
to our own judgment, we need to ask why,

181
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:07,360
And 99.99% of the time you're going to go with the program

182
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:09,360
because it's what makes sense.

183
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:13,360
And I said you know, 102 years of experience is hard to beat.

184
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:16,360
So my biggest piece of advice here

185
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,360
is: stick to the program.

186
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:19,360
You'll never regret it.

187
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,360
Knowledge is power,

188
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:25,360
And know what resources contain.

189
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,360
Now, knowledge is power, right,

190
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,360
Knowledge is good.

191
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,360
So there are a number of resources out there

192
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,360
that you know. you're not going to commit to memory,

193
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:35,360
but you're going to know what they contain

194
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:37,360
And whenever a question arises,

195
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:40,360
then you're going to run to the resource.

196
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:43,360
Just remember that little phrase: run to the resource.

197
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:45,360
Don't substitute your own judgment for it.

198
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:48,360
Go to the resource, mix your judgment with the resource

199
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,360
and stick with the program.

200
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:52,360
That's what's the most important thing.

201
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:55,360
So here's what you'll want to read

202
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:57,360
cover to cover, maybe with a notebook nearby

203
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:59,360
ready to take some notes,

204
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,360
maybe with a highlighter in hand

205
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:03,360
ready to highlight passages.

206
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:06,360
But it's really important to make sure

207
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:09,360
that you've gone cover to cover through the following,

208
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,360
And the first one is the Scout Handbook.

209
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:14,360
Now, get your own copy-

210
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:16,360
It's the 12th edition now-

211
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:18,360
And get to know the handbook.

212
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,360
You can teach yourself a great deal

213
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,360
by just reading the handbook,

214
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,360
And it's remarkable

215
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:26,360
that many adults

216
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:28,360
involved in scouting

217
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:31,360
have not actually read the Scout Handbook.

218
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:33,360
Isn't that something

219
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:35,360
I mean? it really should

220
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,360
be given a thorough going through

221
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:40,360
And you should be reasonably familiar

222
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:42,360
with everything that's in it,

223
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,360
And it will help you immensely

224
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:47,360
When it comes time for the boys

225
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:49,360
to teach a given skill,

226
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:50,360
or something like that.

227
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:52,360
that's our first point of reference.

228
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:54,360
We're going to start with the Scout Handbook

229
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:56,360
and we're going to work with that.

230
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,360
Is it the best single reference

231
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:00,360
for every outdoor skill

232
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,360
and every single thing in the world?

233
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:04,360
Well, of course not.

234
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:06,360
There are books and other resources

